        {
          "id": "4649f765-4a2c-313f-af1f-43f843ed1261",
          "content": {
            "id": "4649f765-4a2c-313f-af1f-43f843ed1261",
            "contentType": "VIDEO",
            "title": "Ericsson's shares soar after profit beat, shrugs off tariffs",
            "description": "<title><body><p>STORY: Ericsson shares soared more than 14% in early morning trade Tuesday.</p><p>Investors bought in after the firm reported a better-than-expected rise in quarterly earnings.</p><p>Income before tax and interest hit $1.62 billion - almost a tenth higher than analyst forecasts.</p><p>The number also excluded restructuring charges at the Swedish telecoms equipment maker\u00a0</p><p>Cost\u00a0savings and a dominant position in North America have helped Ericsson stay ahead of Nordic rival Nokia in the 5G race.</p><p>The group has also outperformed rivals in winning U.S. contracts, mainly a $14 billion deal with AT&T.</p><p>Ericsson also played down the impact of U.S. tariffs.</p><p>While the firm warned no company is immune to the duties, they currently don't see more impact from it going forward.</p><p>Third-quarter net sales were 9% down from a year ago at just under $5.9 billion, but came in above the analysts' expectations.</p><p>However, sales in the Americas slowed down by 8% from last year.</p><p>Ericsson completed the sale of its Iconectiv business in August, which led to a one-off profit gain of almost $800 million.</p><p>Ericsson also announced a five-year partnership with Vodafone Tuesday.</p><p>The plan is to modernize the operator's programmable networks.</p><p>Financial details of the deal weren't disclosed.</p></body></title>",
            "summary": "<body><p>STORY: Ericsson shares soared more than 14% in early morning trade Tuesday.</p><p>Investors bought in after the firm reported a better-than-expected rise in quarterly earnings.</p><p>Income before tax and interest hit $1.62 billion - almost a tenth higher than analyst forecasts.</p><p>The number also excluded restructuring charges at the Swedish telecoms equipment maker&nbsp;</p><p>Cost&nbsp;savings and a dominant position in North America have helped Ericsson stay ahead of Nordic rival Nokia in the 5G race.</p><p>The group has also outperformed rivals in winning U.S. contracts, mainly a $14 billion deal with AT&T.</p><p>Ericsson also played down the impact of U.S. tariffs.</p><p>While the firm warned no company is immune to the duties, they currently don't see more impact from it going forward.</p><p>Third-quarter net sales were 9% down from a year ago at just under $5.9 billion, but came in above the analysts' expectations.</p><p>However, sales in the Americas slowed down by 8% from last year.</p><p>Ericsson completed the sale of its Iconectiv business in August, which led to a one-off profit gain of almost $800 million.</p><p>Ericsson also announced a five-year partnership with Vodafone Tuesday.</p><p>The plan is to modernize the operator's programmable networks.</p><p>Financial details of the deal weren't disclosed.</p></body>",
